# KNOWN GAP — `todo/t1527`. `gg check` ACCEPTS this program and the compiled
# binary SIGSEGVs.
#
# `unify`'s shared-variable coercion arms make `Shared[T]` / `Mutex[T]` /
# `RWLock[T]` transparent to `T`, so a `shared int` may be passed where a
# `Shared[int]` is expected — the arms' own comment says that is what they are
# FOR ("spawned functions that receive the raw wrapper"). Lowering never
# materializes the wrapper, so the callee receives the raw `int64_t` where its
# ABI wants a `void*`.
#
# MEASURED at `8e6b8d2d3`: `gg check` rc 0, "OK: no semantic errors"; the C
# emits `warning: passing argument 1 of 'worker' makes pointer from integer
# without a cast [-Wint-conversion]` ("expected 'void *' but argument is of
# type 'int64_t'"), and the binary is terminated by SIGSEGV (signal 11). The
# `async` + `spawn worker(counter)` spelling — literally the one the comment
# describes — crashes identically, inside `__shared_token_worker`.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE BUILTIN PATH. It has nothing to do with a user type shadowing
# a builtin name (that class is `todo/t0718` / D51, closed on this axis by the
# `coercion_identity/` row set). Measured on BOTH sides of that migration —
# revert the diff, rebuild, re-run: identical crash. Lowering was not touched.
#
# ⚖ INTENDED — AND THE DIRECTION IS AN OWNER ASK (`todo/t1527`). This fixture
# asserts the ACCEPT direction: the program prints `1` and exits 0, matching
# what `gg check` already promises. The defensible alternative is that the
# coercion arms should REJECT this instead, since no `Shared` allocation exists
# anywhere in the program. What is NOT in doubt is that accept-then-SIGSEGV is
# wrong. Same shape as `known_gaps/box_trait_import_flips_accept_reject.gg`
# before D51 ratified its direction.
#
# Asserted by `known_gap_shared_var_coerced_to_wrapper_param_segv`.

void worker(Shared[int] c):
    print(1)

void main():
    shared int counter = 0
    worker(counter)
